commit 01dec7c9bdce3088a6b6b7ab9f2e403458195efb
Author: Tom Vaucourt <34662901+T0mSIlver@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Sep 18 21:45:42 2026 +0200

    VoxtralRealtime streaming: decode the transcript one token at a time (#265)
    
    After every step the stream session decoded its whole token list back to
    text and compared that text with the previous one. Both cost time
    proportional to the transcript, so a session streaming for hours slowed
    down on every step for reasons unrelated to the audio.
    
    `VoxtralRealtimeTranscriptText` appends each kept token's bytes as the
    token is decoded. It keeps the finished text plus the first bytes of a
    character whose remaining bytes have not arrived, at most three. Bytes only
    move into the finished text where a one-pass UTF-8 decoder has finished a
    character, so `text` stays equal to decoding every byte at once,
    replacement characters included.
    
    The step's `Delta.text` keeps its old meaning: the new suffix when the old
    transcript is a prefix of the new one, the whole transcript otherwise. Only
    the last finished character and what follows it can compare differently, so
    only that tail is compared.
    
    `decodeStreaming(_:)` gives way to `streamingTokenBytes(_:)`, and the
    tokenizer's per-token byte lookup becomes internal.
    
    Tests: over 64 random sequences of tokens that split two-, three- and
    four-byte characters, combine accents and emoji, or carry invalid bytes,
    `text` matches a one-pass decode scalar for scalar and every delta matches
    the whole-transcript comparison. End to end, a 20-second stream with such a
    vocabulary produces exactly offline `generate`'s transcript.

